# Memories (doc 62, phase 7): the old days and how the town has changed, childhood and courting, the dead remembered,
# first days in a new place, friends remembering together and arguing over who has it right. Alternatives are written
# in parallel: the second reply answers the second remark. Rules and chains: rules/health_memories7.talk.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Anyone

scene e3_m_childhood_games
when topic=memories
a : Do you remember playing tail-tag in the square? | We used to race the carts to the bridge. | I spent a whole summer digging for treasure behind the chapel.
b[young] : We still play tail-tag! I'm the fastest.
b[old] : We played it with sticks and stones. And the stones won.
b : Every evening till the lamps came on. | Somebody always fell in. | Did you find any?
a : I never caught anybody. Not once. | It was always me. | A button and a dead mouse. Still treasure.

scene e3_m_mischief
when topic=memories
a : When I was a pup I rang the chapel bell at midnight. | We let the miller's geese out once. Took three days to catch them. | I stole a whole pie off a windowsill. Ran with it hot.
b[priest] : Ah. So that was you.
b[quirk=has_opinions_about_everyone_s_grandmother] : Your grandmother would have skinned you.
b : Did they catch you? | Three days! | Burned your paws, I'll bet.
a : My mother did. That was worse. | Some of them never came back. They're still out there. | Worth every blister.

scene e3_m_strict_mother
when topic=memories
a : My mother had a look that could stop a cart. | Mother made us wash our paws before every meal. Every one. | My mother never raised her voice. She didn't need to.
b[parent=yes] : I've got that look now. I practise it on mine.
b : Mine had a wooden spoon. | Clean paws, clean conscience, mine said. | Those are the frightening ones.
a : She'd have liked yours. | And she checked behind the ears. | I still sit up straight when I think of her.

scene e3_m_first_love
when topic=memories
a : Do you remember your first sweetheart? | I wrote a poem for a girl when I was twelve. It rhymed "moon" with "moon". | The first time I fell in love I couldn't eat for a week.
b[widowed=yes] : I married mine. I'd do it all again tomorrow.
b[old] : I remember. I even remember the hat.
b : I'd rather not. | What did she say? | And then?
a : That bad? | She said it was very round. Like the moon. | Then I was hungry again.

scene e3_m_hard_winter
when topic=memories
a : The winter I was ten, the snow came up to the eaves. | Remember the winter the well froze solid? | We burned the chairs one winter. Every last one.
b[old] : That was nothing. The winter before you were born, the river froze with fish in it.
b[young] : I want snow like that!
b : I remember. We dug tunnels. | We ate snow for a month. | And sat on the floor.
a : Tunnels to the bakehouse. | Melted, it tasted of soot. | We were warm, though.

scene e3_m_bread_better
when topic=memories
a : Bread was better when I was young. Thicker crust. | Nothing tastes the way it did. | Apples were bigger, I'm sure of it.
b[baker] : The bread's the same. It's your teeth that changed.
b[old] : Everything was better. Except the medicine.
b : Your paws were smaller, that's all. | Nothing does. | You were smaller.
a : Don't spoil it. | Not even this cheese. | I've thought of that. I don't like it.

scene e3_m_who_remembers_right
when topic=memories
a : It was the spring the bridge fell down. | That was the year your brother married. | You were there. You wore the green hood.
b #argue : It was autumn. Leaves everywhere. | Not my brother. My cousin. | I've never owned a green hood.
a : Spring. There were lambs. | It was your brother. I was at the wedding. | You had a green hood and you lost it in the river.
b : Lambs in autumn, then. | Was I? Then why don't I remember? | ... It was blue.
a?@argue : We'll never agree, will we. | You don't remember because you drank the barrel. | Blue! There. So you did have one.

scene e3_m_nostalgia
when topic=memories
a : I miss the old days. | Things were simpler when we were young. | Nobody makes anything properly any more.
b[honesty=frank] : The old days had no doctors and the plague every other summer. You can keep them.
b[old] : Simpler, aye. And shorter. Most of us didn't see forty.
b : So do I, some days. | Were they? We just didn't know any better. | They never did.
a : Some days. | Maybe so. | Fair point.

scene e3_m_good_riddance
when topic=memories
a : I don't miss being young. Not one bit. | Good riddance to the old days, I say. | People who miss the past didn't live in it.
b[old] : Nor me. My knees miss it, though.
b[drive=content] : I miss some of it. The light in the evenings.
b : Not even the summers? | Hear, hear. | Some of us did, and miss it anyway.
a : The summers were too hot. | Good riddance, and no flowers. | Then you've a short memory.

scene e3_m_forgetting
when topic=memories
a : What was the name of that fellow who kept goats on the roof? | Who was the priest before the one we've got? | There was a tavern on the corner, before. What was it called?
b[quirk=remembers_every_name_ever_heard] : Bartle Gorse. His wife was Nell. His goats were Pip and Old Smith.
b[old] : I knew it a minute ago. It'll come to me at midnight.
b : I've no idea. | A tall one. Very tall. | The something Arms.
a : He must have had a name. | Tall. That narrows it. | They're all the something Arms.

scene e3_m_old_songs
when topic=memories
a : My grandfather used to sing a song about a three-legged fox. | I can still remember the words to my mother's lullaby. | Do they still sing the Harvest Wolf song where you're from?
b[quirk=hums_old_marching_songs] : Sing it, then. I'll hum the march underneath.
b : How did it go? | Sing it. | Every year. Badly.
a : I've forgotten all but the chorus. | Not in the street. | That's the proper way.

scene e3_m_lessons
when topic=memories
a : The priest taught me my letters. Rapped my paws every mistake. | I sat in the chapel learning sums. I still count on my claws. | I never learned to read. Never needed it.
b[scholar] : It's never too late. I'll lend you a primer.
b[priest] : Paws are rapped less now. A pity, some days.
b : Did it work? | Claws are fine. Ten of them. | Neither did I.
a : I can spell "paw". | Ten's all I need. | We managed.

scene e3_m_lost_things
when topic=memories
a : I had a wooden wolf when I was small. I wonder where it went. | My father carved me a boat. I sailed it in the ditch. | I still have the first penny I ever earned.
b[parent=yes] : My pup has one just like it. Chews it every night.
b : Lost in a move, I'll bet. | Did it float? | Spend it, you miser.
a : Somebody's pup has it now. I hope. | Until the ducks got it. | Never. It's my luck.

scene e3_m_last_time_cried
when topic=memories
a : My father cried once in his life. When the old dog died. | I still think about my grandmother's hands. | I can still smell my mother's kitchen. Smoke and onions.
b[warmth=warm] : Those are the things that stay, aren't they.
b[warmth=cold] : Mm.
b : That's a good thing to remember. | Mine had hands like bark. | Mine was bread. Always bread.
a : It is. | Strong hands. Gentle, though. | Every house has its smell.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- The old

scene e3_m_old_lord
when topic=memories a.stage=old
a : When I was a pup, the old lord rode through on a white horse. | The old head of the House knew every tenant by name. | We had a lord once who paid for the chapel roof himself.
b[young] : A real white horse? Was he a giant?
b[self.house] : The House still keeps its own. Mostly.
b : A white horse! | Not now. Now it's the clerks. | Who pays now?
a : Biggest horse I ever saw. Bit my uncle. | Clerks. Clerks and ledgers. | We do. In pennies.

scene e3_m_old_flood
when topic=memories a.stage=old
a : I remember the great flood. Water in the chapel up to the altar. | The year of the flood, we rowed down the high street. | My father saved a pig from the flood. Carried it on his back.
b[young] : Were there fish in the street?
b[old] : I remember. My mother's chickens rode out on a door.
b : Up to the altar! | Rowed? | A whole pig?
a : The priest preached from a ladder. | In a washtub. Me and my brother. | It never forgave him. Bit him every day after.

scene e3_m_old_plague
when topic=memories a.stage=old
a : Do you remember the Grey Year? The sickness? | Half our street went in the coughing summer. | When I was young there was a mange that took the whole town's fur.
b[old] : I remember. I don't talk about it.
b[young] : Is it going to come back?
b : I've heard of it. | I'm sorry. | The whole town?
a : Nobody does, now. That's the strange thing. | Everyone lost someone. | Bald as eggs, the lot of us. We laughed, after.

scene e3_m_old_courting
when topic=memories a.stage=old
a : In my day you courted with a chaperone and a fiddle. | I walked four miles every Restday to see my sweetheart. | Courting was done properly then. Flowers, and asking her father.
b[stage=adolescent] : Four miles? Why didn't you just send a letter?
b[young] : Ew.
b : Who brought the fiddle? | Was she worth it? | Did he say yes?
a : The chaperone. She was very good. | Every step. Every one. | He said "about time".

scene e3_m_old_prices
when topic=memories a.stage=old
a : When I was young you got a whole loaf for a penny. | My first day's wages bought a week's bread. | A pint of ale, a pie and a song for two pennies.
b[merchant] : And you made half as much, I'll bet.
b[young] : What's a pie cost now?
b : And now look. | Them were the days. | Who sang the song?
a : A penny for a crust, now. | Them were the days. And the nights were cheaper. | Me. Badly. That's why it was cheap.

scene e3_m_old_young_ask
when topic=memories a.stage=young b.stage=old
a : What was it like when you were little? | Did they have pups in the olden days? | Were you ever young?
b : Just like now. More mud. | We were the pups. | Once. For a week. It was very busy.
a : More mud! | Did you play tail-tag? | I don't believe you.

scene e3_m_old_to_young
when topic=memories a.stage=old b.stage=young|adolescent
a : When I was your age, I walked to market on my own. | When I was your age I'd been working two years. | When I was your age, we hadn't even got a well. We had a bucket and a river.
b : Uphill both ways? | Doing what? | A bucket?
a : Uphill, in snow, carrying my brother. | Scaring crows. Badly. | A small bucket. And we were grateful.

scene e3_m_old_old
when topic=memories a.stage=old b.stage=old
a : You and I are the last who remember the old mill. | Remember when we were the young ones and they were the old ones? | We've outlived most of them, haven't we.
b : And the miller's daughter. | And now look at us. | Somebody has to.
a : She threw a turnip at you. | We're the old ones. Gods. | Somebody has to tell it right.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Pups and the young

scene e3_m_pup_last_summer
when topic=memories a.stage=young
a : Last summer I caught a frog THIS big. | Remember last winter? I made a snow wolf. | When I was little, I was scared of the dark.
b[young] : That's not that big. Mine was bigger.
b[old] : When you were little. Last spring, was that?
b : That's a big frog. | Did it melt? | And now you're not?
a : It was THIS big. I'm showing you. | It melted on Tuesday. I was sad till Wednesday. | Only a little bit.

scene e3_m_adolescent_little
when topic=memories a.stage=adolescent
a : When I was little I thought the moon followed me home. | I used to be scared of the miller. Now he's just old. | I was a stupid pup. I'm much wiser now.
b[old] : Wiser, at fourteen. I'll write that down.
b[stage=adolescent] : You still are. A stupid pup.
b : It does, a bit. | He was always just old. | Much.
a : It does not. | Old and shouting. | Don't.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Courtship and marriage

scene e3_m_spouses_met
when topic=memories pair.band=spouses
a : Do you remember the first thing you ever said to me? | You trod on my tail at the harvest dance. | I knew the first time I saw you that I'd marry you.
b : I said your hood was on backwards. | You put it under my paw on purpose. | No you didn't. You thought I was my sister.
a : It was. You were right. | I did. Best thing I ever did. | ... Only for a minute.

scene e3_m_how_met
when topic=memories b.married=yes
a : How did you and yours meet, anyway? | Was it love at first sight, you two? | Who asked who?
b[old] : At a dance, a long time ago. The fiddler's still alive.
b : A wedding. Someone else's. | Fourth sight, maybe fifth. | That's a secret.
a : That's how it goes. | That's better. It lasts. | I'll get it out of you.

scene e3_m_widowed
when topic=memories a.widowed=yes
a : I still say "#dear#" to an empty chair. | I still lay out two cups some mornings. | Thirty years and I never once heard my mate snore. I'd give anything to hear it now.
b[warmth=warm] : Tell me about them. I'd like to hear it.
b[old] : I lay two cups too. Then I drink both.
b : I remember. Always smiling. | You'll stop, one day. Or you won't. | I'm sorry.
a : Always. | I don't want to stop. | Thank you.

scene e3_m_mourning
when topic=memories a.mourning=kin|friend
a : I keep remembering little things. The way they laughed. | The last time I saw them, we argued over nothing. | I'd forgotten how much I'd miss them.
b[piety=devout] : The gods keep them now. And so do you.
b[warmth=warm] : Tell me about the laugh.
b : That's how it starts. | They'll have forgotten it already. | It takes us all like that.
a : Is it? | I hope so. | It does.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Friends and kin

scene e3_m_friends_remember
when topic=memories pair.band=friends
a : Remember that night we got locked in the granary? | Remember when you fell in the millpond trying to impress that girl? | How long have we known each other now?
b : I remember the rats. | She married the miller anyway. | Too long, #pet#.
a : Big as cats. | Your loss was the millpond's gain. | And not long enough.

scene e3_m_friends_old_close
when topic=memories pair.band=friends pair.familiarity>=40
a : You're the only one left who knew me as a pup. | Whatever happens, we'll always have that pie we stole. | Do you remember the first thing you said to me?
b : And the only one who won't tell. | That pie was sacred. | "Get off my wall."
a : You'd better not. | I still think about that pie. | And I didn't. I'm still on it.

scene e3_m_siblings
when topic=memories pair.kin=siblings
a : Remember Mother's kitchen? Burnt porridge every morning. | You always got the bigger bowl. | Do you remember the dog we had? Old Tuft?
b : She burnt it on purpose. Said it was healthier. | I was the eldest. | Tuft ate my shoes.
a : She really believed that. | By an hour! | And you cried for a week.

scene e3_m_parent_child
when topic=memories pair.kin=parent pair.elder=a
a : When you were this high you ate a whole candle. | You used to follow me round like a duckling. | You said your first word in the middle of a sermon.
b : I don't remember that. | I did not. | What was it?
a : I do. It was a good candle. | Every step. I miss it. | It was "more". The priest laughed for a week.

scene e3_m_rivals
when topic=memories pair.band=rivals
a : I haven't forgotten what you did at the harvest fair. | You've had it in for me since we were pups. | You won the race by tripping me, and you know it.
b : That was twenty years ago! | Since you broke my sledge. | You tripped over your own tail.
a : Twenty years and I'm still waiting for sorry. | I fixed it. Badly. | I'll never believe that.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Changes in the town

scene e3_m_ridgemere_growth
when topic=memories here.region=ridgemere a.stage=old
a : When I was a pup, the Heights were sheep pasture. | There was no wall round the docks then. Just mud and boats. | The city stopped at the old bridge. Now look at it.
b[quarter=heights] : Sheep! On our hill. I'd never have guessed.
b[from!=ridgemere] : I thought it had always been this big.
b : Sheep! On the Heights! | Mud and boats. Sounds peaceful. | It never stops growing.
a : The sheep had the best view in town. | It stank. It always stank. | Like a pup in spring.

scene e3_m_ridgemere_sump
when topic=memories here.region=ridgemere a.quarter=sump|wharf|docks
a : The Sump was a fish market once, they say. Before the sea moved. | My grandmother remembered when the Sump had gardens. | There used to be a fountain on our street. It's a drain now.
b[quarter=heights] : I didn't know. I don't come down very often.
b : I'd like to have seen that. | Gardens! | A fountain.
a : Now it's just the smell. | Cabbages, she said. Huge ones. | A drain. With ambitions.

scene e3_m_ridgemere_harbour
when topic=memories here.region=ridgemere
a : I remember the night the warehouse burned on the wharf. The Sound glowed red. | The great gale, when the masts came down like trees. | When the first big ship came into the Sound, the whole city went to look.
b[sailor] : I was out on the water that night. Never prayed so hard.
b[old] : I remember. They said it was the end of the world.
b : I heard about that. | Sounds terrible. | Was it that big?
a : You could read by it at midnight. | It was. Three boats lost. | As big as a chapel. With sails.

scene e3_m_lost_mill
when topic=memories here.region=towns
a : I miss the old mill. You could hear it from the whole valley. | There used to be a dance hall over the smithy. | The old oak in the square. They cut it down for a market stall.
b[old] : I courted under that oak. I carved my name in it.
b[young] : What mill?
b : I remember the wheel. | I remember the floor shook. | A shame, that.
a : Round and round. Like the years. | Shook like a drum. Never fell, though. | A crying shame.

scene e3_m_town_changed
when topic=memories b.stage=old
a : Has #here.town# changed much since you were young? | What's the biggest change you've seen here? | Was there a time before the market?
b : Smaller and muddier and everyone knew your business. | The pups. They don't say good morning. | There was a time before the market. Nobody liked it.
a : Some things haven't changed, then. | I'll start saying it. | I'll take your word for it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- First days in a new place

scene e3_m_first_days_city
when topic=memories here.region=ridgemere a.from!=ridgemere
a : My first day in Ridgemere I got lost for six hours. | When I came to the city I'd never seen the sea. | The first week here, I couldn't sleep for the noise.
b[from=ridgemere] : Six hours? You were lucky. Some are still lost.
b : Everyone does. | What did you think? | Bells, carts, gulls.
a : I found my way by the smell of the bakehouse. | I thought it had no other side. | And the wolf next door, singing.

scene e3_m_first_days_town
when topic=memories here.region=towns a.from=ridgemere
a : When I came from the city, the quiet nearly drove me mad. | My first week here, everyone stared like I'd two heads. | I'd never seen a cow up close till I moved here.
b[from!=ridgemere] : You city wolves always say that. Then you never leave.
b : You get used to it. | They still stare. | Bigger than you'd thought?
a : Now I can't sleep in the city. | Still? | Much bigger. And it looked at me.

scene e3_m_from_frosthollow
when topic=memories a.from=frosthollow here.region!=frosthollow
a : Back home in the valley, the snow came in October and left in May. | I miss the pines. And the quiet. Snow's quiet, you know. | Up in Frosthollow, we'd go months without seeing the sun over the ridge.
b[from=frosthollow] : I miss it too. Even the dark. Strange, that.
b : Sounds miserable. | I didn't know that. | How did you stand it?
a : Miserable and beautiful. | It hushes everything. | Lamps, songs and stubbornness.

scene e3_m_from_coldwater
when topic=memories a.from=coldwater here.region!=coldwater
a : I grew up on the Glasswater. You could see the bottom twenty feet down. | I miss mornings on the lake. Mist, and the loons. | My father caught a pike in Coldwater bigger than me.
b[from=coldwater] : The loons. I'd forgotten the loons.
b : Clear as glass? | What's a loon? | Bigger than you as you are now?
a : Clearer. You could count the pebbles. | A bird that sounds like a ghost laughing. | As I am now. He tells it that way, anyway.

scene e3_m_from_oakhaven
when topic=memories a.from=oakhaven here.region!=oakhaven
a : I grew up under trees older than any town. | In the Warmwood, the light comes green through the leaves. | I miss the smell of the woods after rain.
b[from=oakhaven] : My grandmother said every oak in the Warmwood has a name.
b : No streets? | Sounds lovely. | Wet leaves and moss.
a : Paths. Older than streets. | It was. It is. | You smell it once, you never forget it.

scene e3_m_from_mirebridge
when topic=memories a.from=mirebridge here.region!=mirebridge
a : In the fens we'd row to school. Or wade. | The Sallow Fens in the mist at dawn. Nothing like it. | My mother could cross the bog blindfold.
b[from=mirebridge] : Dry feet, as we said. Never once had them.
b : Wade? To school? | I'll take your word for it. | Could you?
a : With the books on our heads. | It's eerie. You'd love it. | Never. I fell in every time.

scene e3_m_from_larkfield
when topic=memories a.from=larkfield here.region!=larkfield
a : On the downs you can see for twenty miles. I miss the sky. | I grew up with the sheep fair every autumn. Best week of the year. | The larks. You don't get the larks here.
b[from=larkfield] : The larks over the downs at dawn. Don't. I'll weep.
b : Twenty miles of what? | What happens at a sheep fair? | We get gulls.
a : Grass and sheep. And more sky. | Sheep. And haggling. And cider. | Gulls aren't the same.

scene e3_m_from_southmeadow
when topic=memories a.from=southmeadow here.region!=southmeadow
a : Back home the orchards blossom in spring. White as snow, and it smells better. | I miss the warmth. I've been cold since I came north. | We'd tread the grapes as pups. My paws were purple till autumn.
b[from=southmeadow] : And the cider after harvest. I miss the cider.
b : Sounds a sight better than here. | You get used to it. | Purple paws!
a : It is. Don't tell anyone here. | I haven't yet. | All the pups had them.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Regions remembered

scene e3_m_frost_cavein
when topic=memories here.region=frosthollow
a : The year the east seam fell in, the whole valley waited at the mine head. | The long dark winter, when the sun didn't clear the ridge for two months. | The avalanche that took the old road. I heard it from my bed.
b[miner] : I was in the west seam that day. We heard it through the rock.
b[old] : I remember. We sang to keep the pups calm.
b : Did they get out? | Two months! | Sounded like thunder, they say.
a : Most of them. Not all. | We forgot what colour things were. | Louder. And slower.

scene e3_m_coldwater_frozen
when topic=memories here.region=coldwater
a : The winter the Glasswater froze, we walked to the island. | Remember the great pike? Took three wolves to land it. | The summer the lake dropped, and we found the old boat on the bottom.
b[fisher] : I was one of the three. It pulled me in. Twice.
b : I was there! We skated. | Three? I heard four. | Whose boat was it?
a : On our backsides, mostly. | Three. And me. So, four. | Nobody ever said. It's still there.

scene e3_m_oakhaven_storm
when topic=memories here.region=oakhaven
a : Remember the great storm, when the old king-oak came down? | The year the woods burned on the east side. We could see it at night. | There used to be a charcoal camp in the clearing. Smoked like a chimney all year.
b[woodcutter] : It took us a whole year to cut that oak up. Every plank went somewhere.
b : The whole town went to see. | I remember the smell. | I remember the black faces.
a : Like a funeral, it was. | Smoke in everything. Even the bread. | And the songs.

scene e3_m_mire_bridge
when topic=memories here.region=mirebridge
a : The year the Long River took the old bridge. We ferried for a year. | Remember the great flood? The water came up to the second step of the chapel. | When I was a pup the fen froze, and we walked out to the eel traps.
b[old] : The old bridge was better. Stone. This new one sways.
b : I remember the ferry. Wet feet every day. | Up to the second step! | Did you get any eels?
a : Wetter than usual, even. | The priest held service in a boat. | A cold one. We ate it anyway.

scene e3_m_larkfield_fair
when topic=memories here.region=larkfield
a : The year a ram got loose in the fair and ran through the cider tent. | The wool price fell, the year I was fifteen. Half the downs went hungry. | I won a pie at the fair for the best fleece. I'd stolen the fleece.
b[herder] : I remember that ram. He was mine. He's never been happier.
b : I was in that tent! | I remember. We ate turnips. | You never!
a : Did you get out? | Turnips and more turnips. | I gave the pie back. Mostly.

scene e3_m_southmeadow_frost
when topic=memories here.region=southmeadow
a : The late frost, when all the blossom went black in one night. | The hot summer. The best vintage anyone can remember. | The year the wasps took the whole pear crop.
b[farmer] : We lost half the orchard. I still remember the smell of it.
b : That was a bad year. | I still have a bottle. | Wasps everywhere. Drunk ones.
a : Bad for everyone. | Open it! | They chased us home.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Days and seasons

scene e3_m_festival_old
when topic=memories here.day=festival
a : The festivals were bigger when I was young. Three days, not one. | My first festival, I ate so much honey cake I was sick in the well. | I danced with my mate at this festival, years back.
b[young] : This one's big! There's a juggler!
b : Three days? | In the well? | I remember.
a : Three days and three nights. | Nobody drank from it for a week. | Feels like yesterday.

scene e3_m_restday_old
when topic=memories here.day=rest
a : As a pup I hated Restday. Sitting still all day in the chapel. | My father always made pancakes on Restday. | We'd walk to the river after chapel. All of us.
b[priest] : You'd sit still now. Wouldn't you?
b : I still hate it. | Every Restday? | I remember that.
a : Don't tell the priest. | Even when there was no flour. | Good days.

scene e3_m_market_old
when topic=memories here.place=market
a : There used to be a wolf here sold pies shaped like hares. | This market used to be half the size. | My grandfather had a stall right where that one is.
b[merchant] : My father had a stall there too. They fought every morning.
b : I remember those pies. | It's twice as loud, too. | Selling what?
a : Best pies in the world. | And twice as dear. | Pots. Nobody needed pots. He sold them anyway.

scene e3_m_tavern_old
when topic=memories here.place=tavern
a : I met my oldest friend over a spilled cup in this very room. | This place used to have a fiddler every night. | I had my first drink in here. Fell off that stool.
b[innkeeper] : That stool's seen worse.
b : Who spilled it? | What happened to him? | That one?
a : Both of us. | Married. Hasn't played since. | That one. It's never been right.

scene e3_m_winter_first_snow
when topic=memories here.season=winter
a : First snow always makes me think of my mother. | When I was small I thought snow was the moon breaking up. | We'd slide down the chapel hill on trays.
b[young] : Can we slide down the hill? Can we?
b : Why's that? | That's lovely. | The priest hated that.
a : She'd catch the flakes on her tongue. A grown wolf. | It is, isn't it. | That's why we did it.

scene e3_m_summer_swim
when topic=memories here.season=summer
a : Summers we swam in the river every day. | I remember long summers. Light till midnight. | One summer I slept out under the stars every night.
b[old] : We swam in everything. Ponds, troughs.
b : The water was so cold. | They don't make summers like that now. | Did it rain?
a : And we didn't care. | Every summer feels shorter. | Once. I stayed out anyway.

scene e3_m_autumn_harvest
when topic=memories here.season=autumn
a : At harvest we'd all ride home on the last cart. | My first harvest I cut my paw on the sickle. Still have the scar. | When I was a pup, harvest supper was the best night of the year.
b[farmer] : We still do. Ride on the cart. Sing badly.
b : I remember. | Show me. | It still is.
a : Hay down the back of your neck. | There. | For some of us.

scene e3_m_spring_lambs
when topic=memories here.season=spring
a : First spring I ever remember, I watched a lamb being born. | My mother planted beans every spring and talked to them. | Spring cleaning. My mother beat the rugs like they owed her money.
b[herder] : I've seen thousands. Still makes me stop.
b : Was it wonderful? | Did they grow? | Mine too.
a : It was messy. And wonderful. | Better than mine ever have. | They did owe her money.

scene e3_m_night_stories
when topic=memories here.time=dusk|night
a : My grandfather told ghost stories at this hour. I never slept. | We used to sit out and count the stars. | When I was small, I thought the dark had teeth.
b[young] : Tell me a ghost story!
b : I loved those. | How many did you get to? | It does. Sometimes.
a : I still can't walk past the old well. | We never finished. | Don't say that.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- By trade

scene e3_m_smith_first
when topic=memories a.job=smith
a : The first shoe I ever made, the horse kicked it off. | My master made me pump the bellows for a year before I touched a hammer. | I remember the first sword I made. Bent like a willow.
b[smith] : Mine too. A year on the bellows. Built my arms.
b : The horse had opinions. | A whole year? | What happened to it?
a : Horses always do. | And I'm glad of it now. | Hangs over my door. For humility.

scene e3_m_guard_first
when topic=memories a.job=guard|soldier
a : My first night on watch I challenged a cow. | I remember my first arrest. A pup who stole an apple. I let him go. | I've stood this gate twenty years. Seen everything come through it.
b[guard] : My first was a hedge. I thought it was moving.
b : Did it answer? | Soft. | Name one thing.
a : It mooed. I took that as a yes. | He's a carter now. Waves every day. | A bear. On a rope. Dancing.

scene e3_m_miner_first
when topic=memories a.job=miner
a : My first day down the mine, my lamp went out. Longest hour of my life. | My father took me down at twelve. I held his tail the whole way. | I remember the day we broke into the old workings. Tools from before anyone.
b[miner] : I'll never forget the first dark. Real dark. Nothing like it up here.
b : What did you do? | At twelve? | Whose were they?
a : Sat still and sang. Somebody heard. | It was how it was done. | Nobody knows. Ghosts, the old ones said.

scene e3_m_farmer_harvest
when topic=memories a.job=farmer|herder
a : The best harvest I ever had, we couldn't fit it in the barn. | I remember the year the rain never stopped. We lost the lot. | My grandfather ploughed this field with an ox called Patience.
b[farmer] : I remember that year. My wheat rotted standing.
b : Where did you put it? | I'm sorry. | Was she patient?
a : In the chapel, for a week. The priest didn't mind. | We got by. Just. | Never once. That was the joke.

scene e3_m_fisher_catch
when topic=memories a.job=fisher|sailor
a : I caught a fish once as long as my boat. Let it go. | My first time out, I was sick over the side all day. | I remember the storm that took my uncle's boat.
b[fisher] : They're all as long as the boat, the ones that got away.
b : Why'd you let it go? | Everyone is. | I'm sorry.
a : It looked at me. | Still am, some days. | We found the oar. Just the oar.

scene e3_m_priest_first
when topic=memories a.job=priest
a : My first sermon, I forgot every word. I talked about bread for an hour. | When I was a novice, I lit the candles and set my sleeve alight. | I've married half this town and buried the other half's parents.
b[piety=devout] : I remember that sermon! It was about bread. It was lovely.
b : Did anyone notice? | Were you hurt? | That's a lot of cake.
a : Everyone went home hungry. | Only my pride. | And a lot of tears.

scene e3_m_healer_first
when topic=memories a.job=healer
a : My first patient was a goat. I was nine. | I remember the first life I couldn't save. You don't forget. | My teacher could tell what ailed you by smelling your breath.
b[healer] : Mine was a carter with a crushed paw. I still dream of it.
b : Did the goat live? | I'm sorry. | Could she?
a : Lived to bite me. | No. You don't. | Mostly. She was wrong about my uncle. He was just drunk.

scene e3_m_merchant_first
when topic=memories a.job=merchant|baker|innkeeper|crafter|cook
a : I remember my first sale. A candle, to an old dear who felt sorry for me. | My first year I nearly lost the shop. Lived on dry bread. | The day I opened, nobody came till sundown.
b[merchant] : My first customer tried to pay me in onions.
b : Kind of her. | And now? | And who came?
a : She came back every week till she died. | Now I live on slightly better bread. | A wolf asking the way. I sold him a hat.

scene e3_m_servant_old
when topic=memories a.job=servant
a : My first mistress had me polish every spoon twice. | I've worked in that house since I was ten. I know where every board creaks. | The old master used to slip me sweets when the mistress wasn't looking.
b[servant] : Mine counted the spoons every night. Every one.
b : Twice? | Useful, that. | He sounds kind.
a : Once for her. Once for her mother. | Especially at night. | He was. The mistress wasn't.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Wealth and character

scene e3_m_rich_humble
when topic=memories a.wealth=rich
a : I started with nothing, you know. One cart and a borrowed mule. | People forget I grew up in two rooms over a tannery. | My father mended shoes. I've not forgotten.
b[wealth=poor|destitute] : And now you've got the rest of us to borrow from.
b : And look at you now. | I'd forgotten. | Good. Don't.
a : The mule's buried in the garden. | The smell never leaves you. | I won't.

scene e3_m_poor_better_times
when topic=memories a.wealth=poor|destitute
a : We had a good house once. Before Father's debts. | There was a time I bought cake without counting. | I had a good coat once. Fur-lined.
b[wealth=rich] : Times change. They'll change again.
b : I didn't know. | Times change. | What happened to it?
a : Nobody does. I don't tell it. | They do. Not my way, lately. | Sold. For bread. Good bread, mind.

scene e3_m_merry_tale
when topic=memories a.humour=merry
a : Did I ever tell you about the time I sold my uncle's goat to my uncle? | My first time drunk, I proposed to a scarecrow. | I once hid from my mother in a barrel. Of pickles.
b : No. Tell me. | Did it accept? | How long?
a : He paid twice what it was worth. He was so pleased to have one. | It's still thinking it over. | A day. I came out a different wolf.

scene e3_m_dreamy
when topic=memories a.drive=content
a : Sometimes I sit and just remember. It's enough. | I remember a particular afternoon, years back. Nothing happened. It was perfect. | I could happily live all of it again, the bad bits too.
b[drive=ambitious] : I'd rather be making new ones.
b : That's a good way to be. | Those are the best. | Even the bad bits?
a : It suits me. | Light, and bees, and a bit of bread. | They make the good ones taste right.

scene e3_m_frank_bad
when topic=memories a.honesty=frank
a : My childhood was miserable and I won't pretend otherwise. | Everyone says my father was a good wolf. He wasn't. | I don't go to the old reunions. They lie about what it was like.
b[warmth=warm] : Then I'm glad you're here now.
b : I'm sorry. | I won't repeat it. | Somebody has to remember it honest.
a : No need. I came through. | Repeat it. I don't mind. | That's me.

scene e3_m_devout_old_priest
when topic=memories a.piety=devout
a : The old priest blessed me as a pup. I still feel it. | My mother never missed a Restday in sixty years. | I remember the night the gods answered me. I'll not say more.
b[priest] : He was a good priest. I learned from him.
b[piety=ordinary] : I missed one or two. Or forty.
b : That's a fine thing. | Sixty years. | I'll not ask, then.
a : He smelled of candle wax and kindness. | Even in the snow. | But it happened.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Quirks

scene e3_m_quirk_same_story
when topic=memories a.quirk=tells_the_same_three_stories
a : Did I ever tell you about the night the miller's cart lost a wheel? | Did I tell you about my uncle and the bear? | Did I ever tell you about the time I met a Warden?
b : You did. | You did. Twice. | Many times.
a : Well. There was a lot of flour. | It was a big bear. | He was very tall.

scene e3_m_quirk_sayings
when topic=memories a.quirk=quotes_old_sayings_for_everything
a : As my grandmother used to say, you can't unboil an egg. | Old saying: a wolf who remembers is a wolf twice. | Like they said in the old days: the past is a good cook but a bad waiter.
b : What does that mean? | I don't know that one. | I've never understood that one.
a : Means what's done is done. | Means you get to live it again. | Nor have I. But it sounds wise.

scene e3_m_quirk_names
when topic=memories a.quirk=remembers_every_name_ever_heard
a : You know, we met before. Ten years ago. You were with a lame dog called Biscuit. | I remember your father. Tall fellow, laughed like a donkey. | You had a sister. Wren? No. Linnet.
b : You've a memory like a ledger. | That's him. Gods. | Linnet! Nobody remembers Linnet.
a : I can't help it. It all sticks. | He was a good sort. | I remember everybody. It's a curse.
